<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand Lassalle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand Johann Gottlieb Lassalle, Lassal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand Lassalle was a German jurist, philosopher and socialist activist, an initiator of the social democratic movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> to a prosperous Jewish family, he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> and had a complex, often antagonistic relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a>. In 1863 he founded the General German Workers' Association, the first independent German workers' party, which sought socialism through state-aided producers' co-operatives won by universal suffrage, and he negotiated secretly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>. He was killed in a duel in 1864; in 1875 his party merged into the Social Democratic Party of Germany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-lassalle.jpg" alt="Ferdinand Lassalle" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philipp Graff · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_Lassalle_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great organisers of the next generations included <b>Ferdinand Lassalle</b> and August Bebel in Germany; Keir Hardie, a Scottish miner who in 1892 became one of the first independent labour members of the British Parliament and helped found the Labour Party; Ben Tillett, Tom Mann and John Burns,…</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The tradition drew on both the reformist socialism of <b>Ferdinand Lassalle</b> and the revolutionary socialism of Marx and Engels, and August Bebel, Wilhelm Liebknecht and Carl Wilhelm Tölcke are counted among its founders in Germany.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-democracy">Social democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinand Lassalle</b> saw the state as an instrument of justice; anarchists and libertarian socialists reject it.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Wrocław on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4937 392 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand Lassalle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 662h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 662h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Its class is, in truth, the class of all humanity, its liberty is the liberty of humanity itself, its sovereignty is the sovereignty of all.&#8221;</p><footer>The Workingmen's Programme, lecture in Berlin, 1862 &#183; tr. E. H. Babbitt, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…in a morally adjusted community there must be added to it solidarity of interests, mutual consideration, and mutual helpfulness in development.&#8221;</p><footer>The Workingmen's Programme, lecture in Berlin, 1862 &#183; tr. E. H. Babbitt, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
